<urn:atm:triage:finding/AW-PROD-R2-001>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "AW-PROD-R2-001" ;
  triage:title "/v1/diagnostics bounded-deadline timeout releases its ConcurrencyLimitLayer admission permit while the detached spawn_blocking query keeps running against the connection pool" ;
  triage:description "arch-ctm, review-phase-aw-prod-readiness-r2 (NO-GO), relayed by fenix (01M1T2V58E7SP6TZC7Q098QY5Z onward); independently confirmed by quality-mgr at integrate/phase-aw@ac1088c82 by direct read of crates/atm-http-runtime/src/diagnostics_route.rs:103-118 and crates/atm-http-runtime/src/lib.rs:592-618. diagnostics_route.rs wraps `tokio::task::spawn_blocking(move || store.query(...))` in `tokio::time::timeout(state.query_deadline, query_future)`; on timeout it maps to SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE and returns immediately, but the underlying spawn_blocking task is NOT cancelled (spawn_blocking cannot be cancelled) -- it keeps running against the DiagnosticTimelineStore/connection pool until it finishes on its own. Independently confirmed: `with_auxiliary_admission` (lib.rs:602-616) applies `ConcurrencyLimitLayer::new(max_connections)` as the OUTER layer around the whole auxiliary router (health+diagnostics), and that layer releases its permit when the wrapped service's future completes -- which happens at the timeout, not when the detached blocking task actually finishes. Net effect: a client that hits the bounded deadline repeatedly can accumulate more concurrently-running diagnostics queries against the pool than max_connections ever intended to admit, defeating the admission control the deadline+limiter pair was built to provide. The existing test (diagnostics_route.rs:394-420, a_query_that_exceeds_its_deadline_is_reported_as_unavailable) only asserts the response status; it does not assert the permit count or that the orphaned worker is bounded. Not a legacy-daemon finding -- atm-http-runtime is the Tokio+Axum target architecture." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-aw" ;
  triage:triageMode "step2_verdict" ;
  triage:category "concurrency-admission" ;
  triage:severity "blocking" ;
  triage:repeatable false ;
  triage:sweepScope "file_only" ;
  triage:status "open" ;
  triage:dispatchReady true ;
  triage:triagedAt "2026-09-06T01:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:foundIn triage:AWProdReadinessR2 ;
  triage:foundAt "2026-09-05T23:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/AW-PROD-R2-001/route-1> ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/AW-PROD-R2-001/admission-1> ;
  triage:triageRecordVersion "1" .

# quality-mgr, 2026-09-06, qa-phase-aw-integration-review-r2 step 2: confirmed
# via direct read, not relayed only. Fix direction (not yet designed by any
# party): either bound the number of in-flight spawn_blocking jobs
# independently of the request-future lifetime (e.g. a semaphore acquired
# before spawn_blocking and released inside the blocking closure itself,
# so the permit tracks the actual worker, not the HTTP response future), or
# make the timeout best-effort-cooperative by having the blocking closure
# check a cancellation flag between rows. Left to the fix-round implementer;
# this record only certifies the defect.
<urn:atm:triage:occurrence/AW-PROD-R2-001/route-1>
  a triage:Occurrence ;
  triage:file "crates/atm-http-runtime/src/diagnostics_route.rs" ;
  triage:line 103 ;
  triage:snippet "let query_future = tokio::task::spawn_blocking(move || store.query(&storage_query));" ;
  triage:status "open" ;
  triage:closed false ;
  triage:branch "integrate/phase-aw" ;
  triage:headSha "ac1088c82" ;
  triage:occursIn <urn:atm:triage:worktree/integrate-phase-aw/ac1088c82> .

